# One-off fill (13 Nov 2026): openers, worked examples, new figures and hand-offs of Chapters 1 to 5 (PROPOSAL.html, Sections 4 to 6)
from fill_lib import *
edit('01-three.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('Take three numbers and write them in every order. Swapping two neighbours moves you from one order to the next, and the six orders, as points, make a hexagon with every corner 120°, the shape DICTO calls a truncated triangle. Every edge of it, and of every solid in this book that is built from orders, is one swap of two neighbouring values; its length is √2 times the gap between the two values swapped.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW worked example, figure, hand-off-->',
  W('the values 1, 2, 4', 'The six orders are (1, 2, 4), (2, 1, 4), (2, 4, 1), (4, 2, 1), (4, 1, 2), (1, 4, 2), each one swap from the next and the last one swap from the first. Swapping 1 and 2 moves a point by (1, −1, 0) or one of its rearrangements, a step of length √2 · 1; swapping 2 and 4 moves it by twice that, √2 · 2. So the sides alternate √2 and 2√2: the 1:2 truncated triangle, the hexagon of the DICTO 1:2 Truncated Octahedron. With 1, 2, 3 both steps are √2 and the hexagon is regular; with 1, 1 + φ, 2 + φ the steps are √2 φ and √2, and the hexagon is golden (Figure {{f:threehex}}).') +
  FIG('three-hexagons.png', 'threehex', 'Three hexagons drawn by the orders of 1, 2, 3; of 1, 1 + phi, 2 + phi; and of 1, 2, 4, each side labelled in red by the two values it swaps and in grey by its length', 'Three truncated triangles, each the six orders of three values. Every side is a swap (red: the two values that change places) of length √2 times their gap (grey). Left, 1, 2, 3: regular. Middle, 1, 1 + φ, 2 + φ: sides in the ratio φ, a golden cut. Right, 1, 2, 4: sides in the ratio 2, the face of the DICTO 1:2.') +
  HAND('Every edge of every permutohedron is a swap of neighbouring values, and its length is the gap swapped; every 120° hexagon is three values. Add a fourth value: does the solid fill space?'))])
edit('02-kelvin.html', [('rep', '<!--NEW chapter-->', OPEN("In 1887 Lord Kelvin proposed the truncated octahedron as the way to divide space into cells of equal volume with the least wall between them; slightly curved, it stood as the best answer for more than a century, until Weaire and Phelan beat it in 1994 with a foam of two kinds of cell. The truncated octahedron is also the solid of all 24 orders of 1, 2, 3, 4, joined by swaps. This chapter reads Kelvin's cell as that solid: its 36 edges are swaps of three kinds, its squares and hexagons are the two ways two swaps can meet, and it is the one solid of its line that fills space alone.") + '''<p>Write the four values 1, 2, 3, 4 in every order, as points of space: (1, 2, 3, 4), (2, 1, 3, 4), and so on, 24 points in all. They lie in the flat three-dimensional space of points whose coordinates add up to 10, and their hull is a solid with 6 squares and 8 regular hexagons, every edge of one length: the truncated octahedron, Kelvin's cell. Each corner has three edges, one for each pair of neighbouring values, 1 and 2, 2 and 3, 3 and 4.</p>
<h2 data-k="kelvin36">Thirty-six swaps of three kinds</h2>
<div class="box"><b>Proposition {{S:kelvin36}}</b> (classical, read as swaps). The 36 edges of the permutohedron of four values are 12 swaps of each of the three pairs of neighbouring values. Its 6 four-sided faces are the places where a swap of the two smallest values and a swap of the two largest are made in either order; its 8 hexagons are the places where three values next in size cycle, 4 for the values 1, 2, 3 and 4 for 2, 3, 4.</div>
<p class="proof"><i>Proof.</i> At every corner one edge swaps each pair k, k + 1 (Chapter {{ch:three}}), so there are 24 · 3 / 2 = 36 edges, and each pair is swapped along 24 / 2 = 12 of them. By Corollary {{s:o1.2}} a 2-face moves either two disjoint pairs, here only {1, 2} with {3, 4}, which gives the four-sided faces, 4!/(2! 2!) = 6 of them (the positions of the two smallest values), or three consecutive values, {1, 2, 3} or {2, 3, 4}, with the remaining value at any of the four positions: 4 + 4 = 8 hexagons. ∎</p>
''' + FIG('kelvin-swaps.png', 'kelvinswaps', "Kelvin's cell three times, each with the twelve edges of one swap in red; one square and one hexagon gold; on the first, every visible corner labelled by its order", "Kelvin's cell as the 24 orders of 1, 2, 3, 4 (left, the visible corners labelled). In each copy the 12 swaps of one pair are red: 1↔2, 2↔3, 3↔4 (hidden ones dashed). The gold square is two swaps far apart, made in either order; the gold hexagon is three values next in size, cycling.") +
 W('at the corner (1, 2, 3, 4)', 'Swapping the first pair gives (2, 1, 3, 4), the last pair (1, 2, 4, 3), and both, in either order, (2, 1, 4, 3): four corners, a square, because the two swaps touch different values and commute. Swaps among 1, 2, 3 go round a hexagon: (1, 2, 3, 4), (2, 1, 3, 4), (3, 1, 2, 4)… no: one swap of neighbouring values at a time, (1, 2, 3, 4) → (2, 1, 3, 4) → (3, 1, 2, 4) → (3, 2, 1, 4) → (2, 3, 1, 4) → (1, 3, 2, 4) → back, six corners, the values 1 and 2 and then 2 and 3 swapped in turn. Counting all the corners, edges and faces: 24 − 36 + 14 = 2, Euler\'s number of every solid without holes.') +
 '''<h2 data-k="kelvinalone">The one that fills space alone</h2>
<p>Spread the four values symmetrically, with gaps x, 1, x, and Kelvin's cell becomes the slider of Chapter {{ch:slider}}, from the octahedron to the cuboctahedron. Every solid of the slider fills space with a partner (Theorem {{s:o2.2}}). Kelvin's cell is the only one that fills space alone, and #100 proves it with Dehn's invariant, the sum over the edges of length ⊗ angle (Dehn 1901), which vanishes for every solid that tiles space by copies of itself (Debrunner 1980): on the sweep from the octahedron through Kelvin's cell and the cuboctahedron to the cube, the invariant is zero only at Kelvin's cell and at the cube (#100, Theorem 1.1; Figure {{f:dehn}}).</p>
<p>The swap explains where the zero falls. The two cells of the two-site pattern behind the slider (Chapter {{ch:bonds}}) change places under μ ↔ 3 − μ, and Kelvin's cell, at μ = 3/2, is the one left in place. On that line the Dehn coefficient of the cell is 12√2(3 − 2μ), a quantity that changes sign under the swap, so it must vanish at the fixed point; and the wall area per cell is the same at μ and 3 − μ and bends down, so it is largest there (studies/2026-11-13-swaps-and-fixed-points, checks K3 and K4, proved there in exact arithmetic). Kelvin's cell is where the swap stands still. This is one of the few places where a law sits at the fixed point of a swap for a stated reason; the study that catalogued the swaps of DICTO's work found that most laws do not.</p>
''' + '''<figure style="max-width:84%;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto"><img src="figures/new/dehn-sweeps.png" alt="Three sweeps of solids with the Dehn invariant plotted beneath each; on the cube-octahedron sweep it is zero only at Kelvin's cell and the cube"><figcaption><b>Figure {{F:dehn}}.</b> The Dehn test along three sweeps (#100, Figure 1). Top: the cube–octahedron sweep, whose octahedron half is the slider of Chapter {{ch:slider}}; the invariant vanishes only at Kelvin's cell (μ = 3/2) and the cube (gold). From #100; home Book Two, <i>Space-Fillers and Lattice Moves</i>, Chapter 1.</figcaption></figure>
<h2 data-k="kelvin13">Kelvin's cell with its neighbours</h2>
<p>In the filling, each Kelvin's cell meets 14 others, 8 across its hexagons and 6 across its squares. #26 builds the thirteen of them that sit round one cell in three layers, 4 + 5 + 4: twelve neighbours, 8 across hexagons and 4 across squares, with the top and bottom squares left open as two windows onto the gold centre (Figure {{f:to13}}). It is one of the seven periodic thirteens of #26, each a cell and twelve neighbours in its honeycomb. Seen through this book, every contact in the block is a face of the solid of orders: a hexagon where three values cycle, a square where two far-apart swaps commute. The same cell also arose in DICTO's work from the other side: rhombic dodecahedra cut in half and rejoined in Polyhedraverse gave a slightly irregular truncated octahedron that still filled space (#58); Chapter {{ch:rd}} follows a later build of that kind to where it leaves the family.</p>
<figure style="max-width:56%;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto"><img src="figures/new/to13-open.png" alt="Twelve cyan truncated octahedra in three layers round a gold one, seen through an open square window"><figcaption><b>Figure {{F:to13}}.</b> The TO-13 block of #26: twelve Kelvin cells round a gold one, 4 + 5 + 4, seen through one of the two open square windows. From #26; home Book Two, <i>Space-Fillers and Lattice Moves</i>, Chapter 4.</figcaption></figure>
''' + HAND("With equal gaps the swaps build Kelvin's cell, which fills space alone, at the one place where the pair swap of its line stands still. What if the gaps, the lengths of the swaps, move?") + '''<h3 class="srch">Sources</h3>
<p class="src">DICTO: #100 The Dehn Test Picks the Lone Tilers (Theorem 1.1, Figure 1); #26 DICTO ATOM-13×13 (the TO-13 block); #58 (the RD halves rejoined); #48, #105; studies/2026-11-13-swaps-and-fixed-points (checks K3, K4). W. Thomson (Lord Kelvin), On the division of space with minimum partitional area, Phil. Mag. (5) 24 (1887) 503–514; D. Weaire and R. Phelan, A counter-example to Kelvin's conjecture on minimal surfaces, Phil. Mag. Lett. 69 (1994) 107–110; M. Dehn, Über den Rauminhalt, Math. Ann. 55 (1901) 465–478; H. E. Debrunner, Über Zerlegungsgleichheit von Pflasterpolyedern mit Würfeln, Arch. Math. 35 (1980) 583–587; G. M. Ziegler, Lectures on Polytopes (the faces of the permutohedron). The reading of the 36 edges by the pair they swap, and of Kelvin's cell as the fixed point of the pair swap, is this book's; the Dehn test is #100's, the parity and concavity checks are the swaps study's.</p>
''')])
edit('03-slider.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('Change how far apart the four values are, keeping them balanced about their middle, and the solid keeps all the cube\'s symmetry while it changes shape: a slider, from the octahedron through Kelvin\'s cell to the cuboctahedron. Every stop on it fills space with one partner, in one way only. And if you listen to the corners as notes on a sphere, the slider falls silent in its fourth tone at exactly one stop, the golden one.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW #94 section, figures, worked example, hand-off-->', FIG('partner-fill.png', 'partnerfill', 'A gold Icosaxes cell at the centre with eight violet glass Unborn Axes cells pulled away from it towards the corners of a cube', 'The pair of Theorem {{s:o2.2}} at the golden stop x = φ, pulled apart: the gold cell is Icosaxes, T(φ), and the eight violet glass cells round it are its partners, Unborn Axes, T(1/φ), at the centres of the cubes of the lattice. Closed up, the two volumes add to the cell of the lattice, 8 per gold site.', 'max-width:66%;margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto') +
 '''<h2 data-k="silent">The silent station</h2>
<p>A solid can be listened to. Give each of its corners, seen from the centre, a unit weight on the sphere and expand in spherical harmonics: the strength Q<sub>l</sub> of each degree l is the solid's harmonic fingerprint, the bond-orientational order of Steinhardt, Nelson and Ronchetti (1983), with which physicists tell cubic, hexagonal and icosahedral order of atoms apart. Cubic solids ring in the fourth degree; icosahedral ones never do. #94 applied it to the sweep P<sub>μ</sub> = C ∩ μC* of the cube and its polar octahedron, of which the slider of this chapter is the octahedron half: T(x) is P<sub>μ</sub> with μ = 1 + x/(1 + x) (#90, Proposition 6), from the octahedron (μ = 1) through Kelvin's cell (μ = 3/2) to the cuboctahedron (μ = 2).</p>
<div class="box"><b>Theorem {{S:silent}}</b> (#94, Main theorem (A), (B)). On the slider the fourth harmonic Q<sub>4</sub> vanishes at exactly one stop, μ = φ, the golden stop x = φ, Icosaxes. There Q<sub>l</sub> vanishes exactly for l = 2, 4, 8, 14 among the even degrees up to 16, the even degrees with no icosahedral harmonic: the 24 corners of Icosaxes are the corners of two icosahedra.</div>
<p class="proof"><i>Proof (#94).</i> For 1 &lt; μ ≤ 2 the corners are the 24 points (±1, ±a, 0) in all orders, a = μ − 1. With the cubic harmonic K<sub>4</sub> = x⁴ + y⁴ + z⁴ − (3/5)r⁴, Q<sub>4</sub> vanishes exactly when the mean of K<sub>4</sub>/r⁴ over the corners does, that is when (1 + a⁴)/(1 + a²)² = 3/5, or a⁴ − 3a² + 1 = (a² + a − 1)(a² − a − 1) = 0; in 0 &lt; a &lt; 1 the only root is a = 1/φ, μ = φ. At a = 1/φ the corners are the cyclic arrangements of (0, ±1, ±1/φ) in both orders, the two icosahedra of the pyritohedral pair, and an icosahedron is silent in every degree that carries no icosahedral harmonic. ∎</p>
''' + FIG('slider-silent.png', 'slidersilent', 'Six solids of the slider from the octahedron to the cuboctahedron, Icosaxes circled in red, above a curve of the fourth harmonic that touches zero only at the golden stop', 'The slider and its silent station. Top: six stops, squares gold and hexagons blue; Icosaxes, the golden stop, is circled. Bottom: Q<sub>4</sub> along the slider, recomputed from #94\'s closed form; it touches zero once, at μ = φ. Kelvin\'s cell, the fixed point of the pair swap T(x) ↔ T(1/x), is not where the slider falls silent.') +
 W('the silent station', 'At μ = φ, a = μ − 1 = 1/φ ≈ 0.618, so a² = 1/φ² ≈ 0.382 and a⁴ ≈ 0.146, and a⁴ − 3a² + 1 ≈ 0.146 − 1.146 + 1 = 0. Exactly: 1/φ² = 2 − φ, so a⁴ − 3a² + 1 = (2 − φ)² − 3(2 − φ) + 1 = φ² − φ − 1 = 0. At Kelvin\'s cell (a = 1/2) the same expression is 1/16 − 3/4 + 1 = 5/16, not zero: Kelvin\'s cell is loud in the fourth tone, as every cubic solid is.') +
 '<p>So the slider carries two special stops for two different reasons. Kelvin\'s cell is where the pair swap stands still, and the laws it carries there (filling space alone, the most wall per cell) are forced by that swap (Chapter {{ch:kelvin}}). The golden stop is where the corners become two icosahedra; the swap study found no swap of this line whose fixed point is φ, so the silence is a law of its own kind, a gap ratio meeting the five-fold symmetry, not a fixed point.</p>\n' +
 HAND('Symmetric gaps give one slider; each stop fills space with its partner in one way only, and at the golden stop the fourth harmonic falls silent. What holds each pair together?'))])
edit('04-bonds.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('Put two kinds of site in space, like the caesium and chlorine atoms of caesium chloride, one at the corners of a cubic grid and the other at the centres of its cubes, and let one kind pull harder than the other. Each site claims the space closest to it in the weighted sense, and the cells they claim are permutohedra of four values. How hard they pull is how unevenly the values are spaced.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW worked example, figures, hand-off-->', FIG('two-sites.png', 'twosites', 'Three panels: a gold cell of an A site and a violet glass cell of a B site beside it, at three weights; the gold cell grows from the DICTO 1:2 shape to Kelvin\'s cell to the DICTO 2:1 shape', 'The two kinds of site and their cells at d = −1/3, 0, 1/3: the A cell gold, one of its B neighbours violet glass, pulled a little apart along the cube diagonal that joins the two sites (dots). At d = 0 both are Kelvin\'s cell; at d = ∓1/3 they are the DICTO 1:2 and 2:1, swapped. In every panel the two volumes add to 8, the cell of the lattice per A site.') +
 FIG('gap-bars.png', 'gapbars', 'A horizontal line for the weight d from -1 to 1 with six named stops, each with a bar of three gaps g, m, g drawn to scale above it', 'The weight is the spacing. On the line −1 &lt; d &lt; 1, each named stop carries its four values as a bar of three gaps g, m, g (gold, blue, gold), with d = (g − m)/(g + m): the middle gap grows to the left (towards the octahedron) and shrinks to the right (towards the cuboctahedron).') +
 W('the values 1, 2, 4, 5', 'The gaps are g = 1, m = 2, g = 1, so d = (1 − 2)/(1 + 2) = −1/3: the A cell is the DICTO 1:2 Truncated Octahedron. Its partner, with −d = 1/3, has gaps in the reciprocal ratio, 2 : 1 : 2, for example the values −5, −1, 1, 5 (gaps 4, 2, 4): the DICTO 2:1. Equal gaps give d = 0, Kelvin\'s cell; gaps φ : 1 : φ give d = (φ − 1)/(φ + 1) = 1/φ³, the golden bond.') +
 HAND('The bond weight is d = (g − m)/(g + m), a ratio of swap lengths, and every edge of every bond cell is a swap. Are all of DICTO\'s solids built by swaps?'))])
edit('05-rd.html', [
 ('rep', '<!--NEW opener-->', OPEN('DICTO cut rhombic dodecahedra in half in Polyhedraverse and put fourteen halves together. The result looks like a permutohedron, with squares where Kelvin\'s cell has squares and hexagons between them, and it is not one: its hexagons have their long sides opposite, and none of its edges is a swap. This chapter proves what DICTO saw in his build and finds the place where the paradigm stops.')),
 ('rep', '<!--NEW figures, worked example, hand-off-->', FIG('rd-apart.png', 'rdapart', 'Left: the chamfered cube as a crystal shell around DICTO\'s fourteen gold Hemi RDs and six teal pyramids. Right: the same pieces pulled apart from the centre', 'DICTO\'s build, closed and pulled apart: fourteen Hemi RDs (gold, three shades to tell them apart) and the six cube pyramids that fill its dents (teal glass), inside the chamfered cube (crystal shell, left). Pieces placed from the exact transforms of DICTO\'s world export (Theorem {{s:o4.1}}).') +
 FIG('hexagons-two.png', 'hexagonstwo', 'Left: a truncated triangle with all six sides red. Right: an elongated hexagon of the chamfered cube with all six sides grey', 'Side by side: the truncated triangle of Kelvin\'s family, every side a swap (red, lengths in units of √2), and the elongated hexagon of the chamfered cube, sides 1, √3/2, √3/2 in turn, none of them a swap (grey). No edge of the chamfered cube runs along a swap direction (1, −1, 0) or its images.') +
 W('the volume ledger', 'The chamfered cube is a zonohedron with seven generators, the three unit axes and the four half body diagonals ½(±1, ±1, ±1). Its volume is the sum of |det| over its triples of generators: 1 (the three axes) + 6 (two axes with one diagonal, ½ each, twelve triples) + 6 (one axis with two diagonals, ½ each... in all 6) + 2 (three diagonals, ½ each, four triples) = 15. DICTO\'s pieces give 14 Hemi RDs of volume 1 and 6 cube pyramids of ⅙: 14 + 1 = 15, the same.') +
 HAND('No: the chamfered cube\'s edges run along directions that swap no two values, and the paradigm stops there. But the swap itself does not stop: inside cells it becomes a trade of neighbours.'))])
